0,Ngiyambaa language,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ngiyambaa_language,"April 22, 2025 - The Ngiyampaa language, also spelt Ngiyambaa, Ngempa, Ngemba and other variants, is a Pama–Nyungan language of the Wiradhuric subgroup. It was the traditional language of the Wangaaypuwan and Wayilwan peoples of New South Wales. Ngiyampaa was the traditional language of the Wangaaypuwan and Wayilwan peoples of New South Wales, Australia, but is now moribund. According to Tamsin Donaldson (1980) there are two dialects of Ngiyampaa: Wangaaybuwan...",Wangaaybuwan-Ngiyambaa,Australian
1,Wangaaypuwan - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wangaaybuwan,"July 31, 2025 - The Wangaaypuwan, also known as ... Australian people who traditionally lived between Nyngan, the headwaters of Bogan Creek, and on Tigers Camp and Boggy Cowal creeks and west to Ivanhoe, New South Wales. They are a clan of the Ngiyampaa nation. The tribal ethnonym derives from their word for ""no"", variously transcribed worjai, wonghi or wangaay. They spoke a distinct dialect of the Ngiyambaa ...",Wangaaybuwan-Ngiyambaa,Australian
